usage:
To use this recipe:
1.  Create a new GDScript file named `memory_shard.gd` and paste the following code into it:
    gdscript
    class_name MemoryShard extends Resource

    @export var id: String = ""
    @export var memory_name: String = "Unknown Memory"
    @export var effect_strength: float = 1.0 # e.g., damage multiplier, shield strength
    @export var visual_color: Color = Color.WHITE # For shader effect on weapon and particles
    @export var particle_texture: Texture2D # For particle system
    @export var narrative_tag: String = "" # For narrative consequences

2.  Create a new Shader file named `mnemonic_catalyst_cannon.gdshader` and paste the following GLSL code into it:
    glsl
    shader_type spatial;
    render_mode blend_mix, depth_draw_opaque, cull_back, diffuse_lambert, specular_schlick_ggx;

    uniform vec4 albedo_color : source_color = vec4(1.0);
    uniform float glow_intensity : hint_range(0.0, 10.0) = 0.0;
    uniform sampler2D texture_albedo : source_color;

    void fragment() {
    	vec4 albedo_tex = texture(texture_albedo, UV);
    	ALBEDO = albedo_color.rgb * albedo_tex.rgb;
    	ALPHA = albedo_color.a * albedo_tex.a;

    	EMISSION = albedo_color.rgb * glow_intensity;
    }

3.  (Optional, for particle effects) Create a new Shader file named `particle_dissipation.gdshader` and paste the following GLSL code into it. This shader should be used for the `process_material` of your `GPUParticles3D` node:
    glsl
    shader_type particles;

    uniform vec4 color_tint : source_color = vec4(1.0);
    uniform sampler2D texture_albedo : source_color;

    void start() {
    	COLOR = color_tint;
    	VELOCITY *= 0.5; // Example initial velocity
    }

    void process() {
    	COLOR.a = 1.0 - CUSTOM_DATA.x; // Fade out over lifetime
    	ALBEDO = texture(texture_albedo, UV).rgb;
    }

4.  In your Godot project, create a new `ShaderMaterial` resource (e.g., `res://materials/weapon_shader_mat.tres`) and assign `mnemonic_catalyst_cannon.gdshader` to its `Shader` property.
5.  (Optional) Create another `ShaderMaterial` resource (e.g., `res://materials/particle_shader_mat.tres`) and assign `particle_dissipation.gdshader` to its `Shader` property.
6.  In your 3D scene, create a `Node3D` (e.g., `WeaponRoot`).
7.  Add a `MeshInstance3D` as a child of `WeaponRoot` (e.g., `WeaponMesh`). Assign a mesh (e.g., a `BoxMesh` or imported weapon model) to it. This will be the visual representation of the cannon.
8.  Add a `GPUParticles3D` node as a child of `WeaponRoot` (e.g., `ShardParticles`). Assign the `particle_shader_mat.tres` (or a `ParticleProcessMaterial`) to its `Process Material` property. Configure its `Draw Passes` to use a `QuadMesh` or similar.
9.  Add an `AnimationPlayer` node as a child of `WeaponRoot` (e.g., `WeaponAnimator`). Create an animation named `fire_animation` that animates the weapon's recoil or glow.
10. Attach the `mnemonic_catalyst_cannon.gd` script to the `WeaponRoot` node.
11. In the Inspector for `WeaponRoot`, assign the `weapon_mesh` property to your `WeaponMesh` node, `particle_system` to `ShardParticles`, `animation_player` to `WeaponAnimator`, and `base_shader_material` to your `weapon_shader_mat.tres`.
12. To use the weapon, create instances of `MemoryShard` resources (e.g., `res://data/memory_courage.tres`). Configure their `id`, `memory_name`, `effect_strength`, `visual_color`, and `particle_texture`.
13. In your game logic (e.g., a player script), get a reference to the `MnemonicCatalystCannon` and call `add_memory_shard(my_shard_resource)` to add memories. Then, call `fire_weapon(index)` to consume a memory and fire the weapon. Listen to the `memory_consumed` and `identity_lost` signals for game state and narrative updates.
